Standard scaffold — just the main class and test class.

Let's start with **Step 1: The Entities**.

Create the package structure. Inside `src/main/java/com/chatApplication/backend/`, make a folder called `entity/`.

In that folder, create **`User.java`**.

Here's what it needs:

```java
package com.chatApplication.backend.entity;

import jakarta.persistence.*;

@Entity
@Table(name = "users")
public class User {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    @Column(unique = true, nullable = false)
    private String username;

    @Column(nullable = false)
    private String password;   // BCrypt hashed — never send this to client

    private boolean online;

    // getters, setters, constructors
}
```

And **`Message.java`** in the same folder:

```java
package com.chatApplication.backend.entity;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import java.time.LocalDateTime;

@Entity
@Table(name = "messages")
public class Message {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    @Column(nullable = false, columnDefinition = "TEXT")
    private String content;

    @ManyToOne
    @JoinColumn(name = "sender_id", nullable = false)
    private User sender;

    @ManyToOne
    @JoinColumn(name = "recipient_id", nullable = false)
    private User recipient;

    private LocalDateTime timestamp;

    @Enumerated(EnumType.STRING)
    private MessageStatus status;   // PENDING, DELIVERED, READ

    // getters, setters, constructors
}
```

And an enum **`MessageStatus.java`** inside entity/:

```java
package com.chatApplication.backend.entity;

public enum MessageStatus {
    PENDING, DELIVERED, READ
}
```

---

A few things to notice before you write:

1. **`@ManyToOne`** on sender and recipient — a Message has one sender and one recipient. A User can have many messages. That's a ManyToOne relationship. Hibernate handles the foreign keys automatically.

2. **`@Enumerated(EnumType.STRING)`** — stores the enum as a string in the DB (e.g. `"PENDING"`) not as a number. Much more readable when you look at the raw table.

3. **`columnDefinition = "TEXT"`** on content — because messages can be long. Without this, JPA defaults to `VARCHAR(255)` which would truncate anything longer.

4. **We're using Lombok?** If you want, slap `@Data`, `@NoArgsConstructor`, `@AllArgsConstructor` on both entities and skip writing getters/setters manually. Your call.

---

Go ahead — create the files. If you hit any confusion about annotations, stop and ask. 🎯
